nvproxy: use the application's host address space for coherent ARM GPUs

Backport of gvisor/gvisor#16643 at f7611cc8354fa081bd8a4152b983c890c57ccec3
by Eric Zhang, with contributions from Ryan Daum. On coherent ARM systems
such as GB200, forcing UVM multi-process sharing disables the address-space
association required by ATS. In systrap, initialize UVM against the
application's host address space and prepare its mappings before the GPU
accesses pageable memory. Reject a live checkpoint that would retain a UVM
descriptor bound to the old address space.

Apply after Orbit's per-GPU memory limit (0004). The UVM MM file still
holds its tracked VA-space file on successful initialization, and restore
recreates both the destination UVM modes and the device-memory accounting.
Include the UVM tools descriptor guard and ARM-only ioctl policy from Ryan
Daum's review fixes (rdaum/gvisor commits 57f294f7e780 and 8a0ffa284f18).
A failed systrap transport returns an I/O error to the application.

The upstream patch was tested on a rootless GB200 with 64 KiB host pages
and NVIDIA driver 610.57.04 (gvisor/gvisor#18027).

